        <div class="content">

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">INTRODUCTION</div>
                <p>Apps, as an abbreviation of mobile applications, are software products for mobile platforms (Tang, 2016). They are designed to perform certain tasks on mobile devices (Islam, Islam, &amp; Mazumder, 2010). This product category has grown to become a gigantic differentiated market with over 3.48 million apps on Google Play, an app marketplace, and the recent projection of over 935-billion-dollar revenue in 2023 (Statista Research, 2021a, Statista Research, 2021b).</p>
            </div>

            <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
            <div class="section">
                <div class="section-title">OBJECTIVE</div>
                <p>This work studies the adverse effect of “Free App of the Day” at Amazon Appstore.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- METHODOLOGY + RESULTS (SIDE-BY-SIDE) -->
            <div class="methodology-results">

                <!-- METHODOLOGY COLUMN -->
                <div class="column">
                    <div class="section">
                        <div class="section-title">METHODOLOGY</div>

                        <div class="section">
                            <div class="section-title">1. Literature review</div>
                            <ul>
                                <li>a. Digital free sampling</li>
                                <li>b. eWOM</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="section">
                            <div class="section-title">2. Hypotheses</div>
                            <ul>
                                <li>a. H1 - Online user decisions to adopt apps are discouraged by the price rebound after the zero-price offering of FAD ends. The sharper price rebound leads to a greater decrease in app adoptions.</li>
                                <li>b. H2 - Conditional on the total volume of eWOM, organic reviews are more influential to online user decisions of adopting apps than promotion-induced reviews after the FAD promotion ends.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="section">
                            <div class="section-title">3. Context and data collection</div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="section">
                            <div class="section-title">4. Empirical analysis</div>
                            <ul>
                                <li>a. Exploratory analysis</li>
                                <li>b. Regression model and results</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- RESULTS COLUMN -->
                <div class="column">
                    <div class="section">
                        <div class="section-title" style="color:#c8102e;">RESULTS</div>
                        <p>We notice a few apps were off the direct spotlight post-promotion; however, they still offer zero price for another one or two days. Specifically, an increase in SkewDailyi indicates a more right skewed distribution of daily number of reviews during the post-promotion period.</p>

